Lab 15: Advanced Performance Tuning with Blktrace
Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Install and configure blktrace and related tools for block I/O tracing
Capture and analyze block layer I/O activity using blktrace and blkparse
Interpret blktrace/blkparse output to identify performance bottlenecks
Use btt (blktrace's companion tool) to generate queue and completion statistics
Analyze I/O patterns, throughput, and latency metrics
Configure I/O schedulers and tune kernel queue parameters for better performance
Monitor the impact of tuning changes using before/after comparisons
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic understanding of Linux file systems and storage concepts
Familiarity with command-line operations and text editors
Knowledge of I/O concepts including throughput, latency, and IOPS
Understanding of Linux kernel modules and system administration
Experience with performance monitoring tools like iostat and iotop
Root or sudo access to perform system-level configurations
Install the required packages before beginning:

For RHEL/CentOS systems:

sudo yum update -y
sudo yum install -y blktrace sysstat iotop
For Ubuntu/Debian systems:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y blktrace sysstat iotop
blktrace ships with two companion binaries used throughout this lab: blkparse (converts binary traces into readable text) and btt (aggregates parsed traces into queue/completion/latency statistics). Both are installed automatically with the blktrace package.

