Verify the installation and identify your primary block device before proceeding:

# Check blktrace version
blktrace -V

# List available block devices
lsblk

# Mount debugfs if not already mounted (blktrace requires it)
mount | grep debugfs || sudo mount -t debugfs debugfs /sys/kernel/debug

# Identify the primary storage device
PRIMARY_DEVICE=$(lsblk -d -o NAME --noheadings | head -1)
echo "Primary device: $PRIMARY_DEVICE"

# Check current I/O scheduler for the primary device
cat /sys/block/$PRIMARY_DEVICE/queue/scheduler
Create the working directory and test files used throughout the lab:

sudo mkdir -p /opt/blktrace-lab
sudo chown $USER:$USER /opt/blktrace-lab
cd /opt/blktrace-lab

# Create test files of different sizes
dd if=/dev/zero of=test_1mb.dat bs=1M count=1
dd if=/dev/zero of=test_10mb.dat bs=1M count=10
dd if=/dev/zero of=test_100mb.dat bs=1M count=100
Deliverable for setup: lsblk output showing at least one block device, and confirmation that /opt/blktrace-lab contains test_1mb.dat, test_10mb.dat, and test_100mb.dat (verify with ls -lh /opt/blktrace-lab).

Task 1: Capture Block I/O Activity with Blktrace
Subtask 1.1: Run a Basic Trace While Generating I/O
Start a trace, generate I/O against the test files, and stop the trace cleanly.

cd /opt/blktrace-lab

# Start a basic trace in the background
sudo blktrace -d /dev/$PRIMARY_DEVICE -o trace_basic &
TRACE_PID=$!

# Give the trace a moment to attach
sleep 1

# Generate I/O activity: sequential write, then sequential read
dd if=/dev/zero of=/opt/blktrace-lab/trace_test.dat bs=1M count=50 conv=fsync
dd if=/opt/blktrace-lab/trace_test.dat of=/dev/null bs=64k

# Stop the trace
sleep 1
sudo kill -INT $TRACE_PID
wait $TRACE_PID 2>/dev/null

# List generated per-CPU trace files
ls -la trace_basic.blktrace.*
blktrace writes one binary file per CPU core (e.g. trace_basic.blktrace.0, trace_basic.blktrace.1, ...). This is expected and required by blkparse.

Deliverable: output of ls -la trace_basic.blktrace.* showing one or more non-empty binary trace files (size greater than 0 bytes).

Subtask 1.2: Parse the Trace into Human-Readable Format
Convert the binary trace data with blkparse and inspect the event stream.

cd /opt/blktrace-lab

# Parse all per-CPU files belonging to the trace_basic run into a text file
blkparse -i trace_basic -o trace_basic_parsed.txt

echo "=== FIRST 20 PARSED EVENTS ==="
head -20 trace_basic_parsed.txt

echo "=== TOTAL PARSED LINES ==="
wc -l trace_basic_parsed.txt

echo "=== EVENT TYPE COUNTS (Q=queued, D=issued to driver, C=completed) ==="
awk '{print $6}' trace_basic_parsed.txt | grep -E '^[A-Z]$' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn

echo "=== READ vs WRITE OPERATIONS ==="
grep -c ' R ' trace_basic_parsed.txt
grep -c ' W ' trace_basic_parsed.txt
Each parsed line follows the format <major,minor> <cpu> <seqnum> <timestamp> <pid> <action> <rwbs> <sector> + <nr_sectors> [<process>]. The single-letter action code in field 6 (Q, G, I, D, C, etc.) identifies the stage of the request lifecycle; D marks dispatch to the driver and C marks completion, which is what latency calculations in Task 2 rely on.

Deliverable: trace_basic_parsed.txt must exist and contain more than zero lines; the action-type count table must show at least one C (complete) entry, confirming that I/O completions were captured.

Task 2: Generate Queue and Latency Statistics with btt
blkparse shows raw events, but computing queue depth, dispatch latency, and completion latency by hand from raw text is error-prone. The correct tool for aggregate statistics is btt, which consumes the same binary trace files and produces a structured summary. (Note: blkparse itself has no -q/quiet statistics flag â�� the standard way to obtain queue/completion statistics is btt.)

Subtask 2.1: Run a Realistic Mixed Workload Under Trace
cd /opt/blktrace-lab

# Start tracing with explicit action mask for queue, issue, and complete events
sudo blktrace -d /dev/$PRIMARY_DEVICE -o trace_mixed -a queue -a issue -a complete &
TRACE_PID=$!
sleep 1

# Sequential write
dd if=/dev/zero of=/opt/blktrace-lab/mixed_write.dat bs=64k count=800 conv=fsync &
WRITE_PID=$!

# Sequential read
dd if=/opt/blktrace-lab/test_100mb.dat of=/dev/null bs=64k &
READ_PID=$!

# Random small reads
for i in $(seq 1 50); do
    dd if=/opt/blktrace-lab/test_100mb.dat of=/dev/null bs=4k count=1 \
       skip=$((RANDOM % 1000)) 2>/dev/null &
done

wait $WRITE_PID $READ_PID
sleep 2

sudo kill -INT $TRACE_PID
wait $TRACE_PID 2>/dev/null

rm -f /opt/blktrace-lab/mixed_write.dat

ls -la trace_mixed.blktrace.*
Deliverable: ls -la trace_mixed.blktrace.* shows non-empty per-CPU trace files for the trace_mixed run.

Subtask 2.2: Parse and Aggregate with blkparse and btt
cd /opt/blktrace-lab

# Step 1: blkparse combines the per-CPU binary files into a single
# text stream and (with -d) a binary "dump" file that btt can consume directly.
blkparse -i trace_mixed -d trace_mixed.bin -o trace_mixed_parsed.txt

echo "=== PARSED EVENT COUNT ==="
wc -l trace_mixed_parsed.txt

# Step 2: btt reads the combined binary dump and produces aggregate
# queue-depth, dispatch-latency, and completion-latency statistics.
btt -i trace_mixed.bin > btt_summary.txt 2>&1

echo "=== BTT SUMMARY (Q2C = time from queue to completion) ==="
cat btt_summary.txt
Inspect btt_summary.txt. It contains several report sections; the most important for tuning decisions are:

==================== All Devices ==================== with a table of Q2Q, Q2G, G2I, Q2M, I2D, D2C, and Q2C average times â�� these represent, respectively, time between queue events, queue-to-get-request, get-to-issue, queue-to-merge, issue-to-dispatch, dispatch-to-completion, and total queue-to-completion latency.
Device Overhead showing what fraction of total latency is spent in each stage.
A per-process or per-region breakdown depending on the options used.
echo "=== EXTRACTING KEY LATENCY METRICS ==="
grep -A 12 "All Devices" btt_summary.txt

echo "=== EXTRACTING Q2C (TOTAL LATENCY) LINE ==="
grep "Q2C" btt_summary.txt
Deliverable: btt_summary.txt exists and is non-empty, contains an All Devices section, and includes at least one numeric Q2C average latency value (in seconds, e.g. 0.001234). This file is the concrete artifact used in Task 3 to justify tuning decisions.

Task 3: Tune I/O Scheduler and Queue Parameters, Then Validate
Subtask 3.1: Enumerate Available Schedulers Reliably
The /sys/block/<dev>/queue/scheduler file lists available schedulers with the active one in square brackets, e.g. mq-deadline [kyber] bfq none. Parsing this reliably (rather than assuming tr '[]' always behaves the same across shells) is done with a portable sed/grep combination:

cd /opt/blktrace-lab

SCHED_FILE="/sys/block/$PRIMARY_DEVICE/queue/scheduler"

echo "=== RAW SCHEDULER FILE CONTENTS ==="
cat "$SCHED_FILE"

echo "=== CURRENTLY ACTIVE SCHEDULER ==="
grep -oP '(?<=\[)[^]]+(?=\])' "$SCHED_FILE" || \
    sed -n 's/.*\[\([a-z-]*\)\].*/\1/p' "$SCHED_FILE"

echo "=== ALL AVAILABLE SCHEDULERS (ONE PER LINE) ==="
sed 's/\[//; s/\]//' "$SCHED_FILE" | tr ' ' '\n' | grep -v '^$' | tee available_schedulers.txt
This produces available_schedulers.txt with one scheduler name per line (for example mq-deadline, kyber, bfq, none), regardless of which ones happen to be compiled in on this system. Every subsequent step reads from this file instead of assuming a fixed list, so the lab works correctly whether the kernel exposes two schedulers or five.

Deliverable: available_schedulers.txt exists and contains at least one scheduler name matching one of the tokens originally printed in $SCHED_FILE.

Subtask 3.2: Benchmark Every Available Scheduler
cd /opt/blktrace-lab

echo "device,scheduler,seq_read_MBps,seq_write_MBps" > scheduler_results.csv

while read -r SCHED; do
    [ -z "$SCHED" ] && continue
    echo "--- Testing scheduler: $SCHED on $PRIMARY_DEVICE ---"

    # Apply the scheduler; skip if the kernel rejects it
    if ! echo "$SCHED" | sudo tee /sys/block/$PRIMARY_DEVICE/queue/scheduler >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "Scheduler $SCHED could not be applied, skipping."
        continue
    fi
    sleep 1

    # Drop caches so reads are measured from disk, not page cache
    sync
    echo 3 | sudo tee /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

    READ_OUT=$(dd if=/opt/blktrace-lab/test_100mb.dat of=/dev/null bs=64k 2>&1)
    READ_MBPS=$(echo "$READ_OUT" | grep -oE '[0-9.]+ MB/s' | grep -oE '^[0-9.]+')
    [ -z "$READ_MBPS" ] && READ_MBPS=$(echo "$READ_OUT" | grep -oE '[0-9.]+ GB/s' | grep -oE '^[0-9.]+' | awk '{print $1*1024}')

    WRITE_OUT=$(dd if=/dev/zero of=/opt/blktrace-lab/sched_write.dat bs=64k count=1000 conv=fsync 2>&1)
    WRITE_MBPS=$(echo "$WRITE_OUT" | grep -oE '[0-9.]+ MB/s' | grep -oE '^[0-9.]+')
    [ -z "$WRITE_MBPS" ] && WRITE_MBPS=$(echo "$WRITE_OUT" | grep -oE '[0-9.]+ GB/s' | grep -oE '^[0-9.]+' | awk '{print $1*1024}')

    rm -f /opt/blktrace-lab/sched_write.dat

    echo "$PRIMARY_DEVICE,$SCHED,${READ_MBPS:-NA},${WRITE_MBPS:-NA}" >> scheduler_results.csv

done < available_schedulers.txt

echo "=== SCHEDULER BENCHMARK RESULTS ==="
column -s, -t scheduler_results.csv
Deliverable: scheduler_results.csv contains a header row plus one data row per scheduler listed in available_schedulers.txt, each with a device name, scheduler name, and numeric (or NA) throughput values. Displaying it with column -s, -t should render a readable table with no blank scheduler names.

Subtask 3.3: Tune Queue Depth and Read-Ahead, Then Validate with btt
Pick the best-performing scheduler from scheduler_results.csv and apply it, then tune nr_requests and read_ahead_kb.

cd /opt/blktrace-lab

# Example: apply mq-deadline (replace with your best-performing scheduler from Subtask 3.2)
BEST_SCHED="mq-deadline"
echo "$BEST_SCHED" | sudo tee /sys/block/$PRIMARY_DEVICE/queue/scheduler

echo "device,nr_requests,read_ahead_kb,seq_read_MBps" > queue_tuning_results.csv

for QD in 32 128 256; do
    for RA in 128 512 2048; do
        echo "$QD" | sudo tee /sys/block/$PRIMARY_DEVICE/queue/nr_requests >/dev/null
        echo "$RA" | sudo tee /sys/block/$PRIMARY_DEVICE/queue/read_ahead_kb >/dev/null
        sync
        echo 3 | sudo tee /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

        OUT=$(dd if=/opt/blktrace-lab/test_100mb.dat of=/dev/null bs=64k 2>&1)
        MBPS=$(echo "$OUT" | grep -oE '[0-9.]+ MB/s' | grep -oE '^[0-9.]+')
        echo "$PRIMARY_DEVICE,$QD,$RA,${MBPS:-NA}" >> queue_tuning_results.csv
    done
done

echo "=== QUEUE/READ-AHEAD TUNING RESULTS ==="
column -s, -t queue_tuning_results.csv
Apply the best combination found (replace 128 and 512 with your measured optimum), then re-run a trace and confirm the improvement with btt:

BEST_QD=128
BEST_RA=512
echo "$BEST_QD" | sudo tee /sys/block/$PRIMARY_DEVICE/queue/nr_requests
echo "$BEST_RA" | sudo tee /sys/block/$PRIMARY_DEVICE/queue/read_ahead_kb

# Re-run the same mixed workload trace as Task 2 under the new configuration
sudo blktrace -d /dev/$PRIMARY_DEVICE -o trace_tuned -a queue -a issue -a complete &
TRACE_PID=$!
sleep 1

dd if=/dev/zero of=/opt/blktrace-lab/tuned_write.dat bs=64k count=800 conv=fsync &
WPID=$!
dd if=/opt/blktrace-lab/test_100mb.dat of=/dev/null bs=64k &
RPID=$!
for i in $(seq 1 50); do
    dd if=/opt/blktrace-lab/test_100mb.dat of=/dev/null bs=4k count=1 \
       skip=$((RANDOM % 1000)) 2>/dev/null &
done
wait $WPID $RPID
sleep 2
sudo kill -INT $TRACE_PID
wait $TRACE_PID 2>/dev/null
rm -f /opt/blktrace-lab/tuned_write.dat

blkparse -i trace_tuned -d trace_tuned.bin -o trace_tuned_parsed.txt
btt -i trace_tuned.bin > btt_summary_tuned.txt 2>&1

echo "=== BASELINE Q2C (from Task 2) ==="
grep "Q2C" btt_summary.txt

echo "=== TUNED Q2C (after scheduler/queue changes) ==="
grep "Q2C" btt_summary_tuned.txt
Deliverable: two files, btt_summary.txt (baseline, from Task 2) and btt_summary_tuned.txt (post-tuning), both containing a Q2C average latency value. The student should record both numbers side by side; a successful tuning pass shows the tuned Q2C average latency equal to or lower than the baseline for the sequential-read-dominated workload, or documents a clear tradeoff if it did not improve (for example, higher throughput at the cost of slightly higher average latency under mixed load).

Troubleshooting Tips
Issue 1: Blktrace fails to start with "Permission denied" or "No such file or directory"

# Confirm debugfs is mounted; blktrace requires it
mount | grep debugfs
sudo mount -t debugfs debugfs /sys/kernel/debug

# Confirm the device path exists
ls -la /dev/$PRIMARY_DEVICE
Issue 2: blkparse reports "Invalid input file" or produces zero lines

# Confirm the per-CPU trace files exist and are non-empty for the given prefix
ls -la trace_basic.blktrace.*

# blkparse needs the same -i prefix used with blktrace -o; verify no typo
blkparse -i trace_basic -o /tmp/check.txt
wc -l /tmp/check.txt
Issue 3: btt reports "bno_read: Invalid device"

This happens when btt is pointed at the wrong input. It must consume the binary dump file created by blkparse -d, not the raw per-CPU .blktrace.* files and not the parsed text output.

# Regenerate the binary dump explicitly
blkparse -i trace_mixed -d trace_mixed.bin -o /dev/null
btt -i trace_mixed.bin
Issue 4: High system load or excessive latency during trace capture

If background dd processes from earlier subtasks are still running, they will inflate every subsequent measurement.

# Check for stray dd or blktrace processes before starting a new test
ps aux | grep -E 'dd |blktrace' | grep -v grep

# Kill any leftovers
pkill -f "dd if=/opt/blktrace-lab" 2>/dev/null
sudo pkill blktrace 2>/dev/null

# Confirm system load has settled before re-running a benchmark
uptime
If load remains high after cleanup, check for unrelated background jobs (backups, package updates, or other students' processes on a shared machine) with iotop -o and iostat -x 1 5 before repeating the trace, since contention from unrelated processes will invalidate the before/after comparison in Subtask 3.3.

Expected Outcomes
A complete set of blktrace binary traces, blkparse text output, and btt aggregate statistics files (btt_summary.txt, btt_summary_tuned.txt) demonstrating the full I/O tracing and analysis pipeline
A scheduler_results.csv and queue_tuning_results.csv showing measured throughput across every scheduler and queue/read-ahead combination available on the lab device
A documented before/after comparison of Q2C latency showing the measurable effect of scheduler and queue-parameter tuning on the same workload
Conclusion
In this lab, students built a complete block I/O performance analysis workflow using blktrace, blkparse, and btt, moving from raw binary trace capture through parsed event inspection to aggregate queue and completion latency statistics. Rather than relying on non-existent blkparse flags or hardcoded scheduler lists, the lab used the correct tool for aggregate statistics (btt) and a portable scheduler-discovery method that adapts to whatever schedulers a given kernel actually exposes. By benchmarking every available I/O scheduler and sweeping queue depth and read-ahead settings against a consistent mixed workload, students produced concrete, comparable CSV and btt-summary artifacts that quantify the real impact of each tuning decision rather than relying on guesswork. Going forward, students should apply this same trace-measure-tune-reverify cycle against production-representative workloads, validate changes with fio for more rigorous synthetic benchmarking, and make any beneficial scheduler or queue settings persistent through udev rules or systemd services before relying on them in a live environment.
